{
  "id": "s01-l01",
  "unit": "s01-vowels",
  "title": "Vowels 1: অ আ",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "**Bengali has 11 vowel letters (স্বরবর্ণ *shorborno*).** They stand alone when a vowel *starts* a syllable, e.g. at the start of a word. Here are the first two:\n\n- **অ** = `o`, a short open 'o' as in *or*. Every consonant secretly carries this sound, so you will see it everywhere later.\n- **আ** = `a` as in *father*. It is অ plus an extra stick (া), and that stick is exactly the sign that adds 'a' to a consonant later.\n\nBoth hang from a top line (মাত্রা *matra*), which is how Bengali letters join into words. Like an alphabet chart, each letter comes with words that start with it: **অ**জগর, **আ**ম.",
  "vocab": [
    {
      "id": "l-o",
      "bn": "অ",
      "tr": "o",
      "en": "o as in 'or' (short, open)",
      "notes": "Name: sworo-o (স্বরে অ). Also the 'inherent vowel' hidden in every consonant."
    },
    {
      "id": "l-a",
      "bn": "আ",
      "tr": "a",
      "en": "a as in 'father'",
      "notes": "Name: sworo-a (স্বরে আ). Looks like অ with an extra stroke (া) on the right."
    },
    {
      "id": "ojogor",
